# Fanta Edit

Fanta is a macOS design editor built as a fork of Zed. Package `zed` builds the `fanta` binary; most inherited IDE features are compiled out. When touching inherited Zed code, prefer upstream-compatible seams over rewrites.

| Area | Crates |
| --- | --- |
| Canvas app: load/save, views, panels, Metal canvas, agent surface | `crates/fig_viewer` |
| Document model, operations, history | `crates/fanta-doc` |
| Project format, FNX source | `crates/fanta-format`, `crates/fanta-fnx` |
| Rendering (Skia Graphite on Metal), text | `crates/fanta-render`, `crates/fanta-text` |
| Viewport math, tools | `crates/fanta-canvas`, `crates/fanta-tools` |
| `.fig` import, prototype playback | `crates/fanta-fig-interop`, `crates/fanta-present` |
| Agent seam and built-in canvas tools | `crates/design_surface`, `crates/agent/src/tools/design_*` |
| Shared Fanta UI | `crates/fanta_ui` |

GPUI and the reusable Zed UI crates are not in this repository. They come from `squidred-dev/fanta-ui`, pinned by git revision in `Cargo.toml`; read their source under `~/.cargo/git/checkouts/fanta-ui-*/<rev>/crates/`. `vendor/skia-bindings-0.153.3` carries Fanta's Graphite shim (see its `FANTA-PROVENANCE.txt`).

## Crate rules

Crate rules record traps that earlier changes hit. Claude Code loads them when it opens files in the crate; Codex does not, so open the file yourself before changing one of these areas:

- `crates/fig_viewer/AGENTS.md`
- `crates/fanta-doc/AGENTS.md`
- `crates/fanta-format/AGENTS.md`
- `crates/fanta-fnx/AGENTS.md`
- `crates/fanta-render/AGENTS.md`
- `crates/design_surface/AGENTS.md`: also covers `crates/agent/src/tools/design_*`, `fig_viewer`'s `agent_surface.rs` and `live_mcp.rs`, and the guide seeded into new projects
- `docs/AGENTS.md`: writing docs

## Build, test and verify

- Build in this checkout's own `target/` (Cargo's default). In a worktree, never point `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` or `--target-dir` at the main checkout's `target/`: the user's editor builds there, and you will wait on its lock. A fresh checkout compiles Skia from source (upstream prebuilt binaries lack the Graphite shim), so start the first build in the background.
- Scope commands to what you touched: `cargo test --locked -p <crate> [filter]`, `cargo check --locked -p zed -p cli` for the app, `./script/clippy -p <crate>` for lints (same flags as CI). Without `-p`, `./script/clippy` runs a full-workspace release build that takes over 20 minutes.
- `./script/verify-fanta-release --list` prints the release suites (engine, import, source-order, editor, workspace, ui, build, lint). Run the stages that cover your change before calling it verified; the `fanta-verify` skill maps changes to stages, probes and smoke tests.
- CI (`.github/workflows/check.yml`) requires 51 editor and workspace regressions by exact test name. If you rename or delete one of those tests, update the list in `check.yml` in the same change.
- Regenerate golden images (`FANTA_REGEN_GOLDEN`, `UPDATE_BASELINE`) only when the user asks: a regenerated baseline turns a visual regression into a pass.

## Fanta traps

- Ids have two spellings. Persisted maps (scene nodes, component maps, sidecars) key ids by their serde form, a bare ULID. `Display` and `FromStr` add a type prefix (`n_`, `c_`, `a_`, …), which is what file names, logs, agents and MCP input use. Derive persisted keys through serde (`serde_json::to_value(id)`, or `json_key`/`id_from_key` in fanta-format), never `to_string()`/`from_str`. Four separate PRs hit this. (#85, #102, #110, #128)
- Before deleting an asset, search non-Rust consumers as well: license scripts, Nix font directories and `include_bytes!` paths. (#15)
- When removing a `#[cfg(...)]`-gated `use`, remove its attribute too; otherwise the attribute silently gates the next import. (#16)

# Rust coding guidelines

* Prioritize code correctness and clarity. Speed and efficiency are secondary priorities unless otherwise specified.
* Do not write organizational or comments that summarize the code. Comments should only be written in order to explain "why" the code is written in some way in the case there is a reason that is tricky / non-obvious.
* Prefer implementing functionality in existing files unless it is a new logical component. Avoid creating many small files.
* Avoid using functions that panic like `unwrap()`, instead use mechanisms like `?` to propagate errors.
* Be careful with operations like indexing which may panic if the indexes are out of bounds.
* Never silently discard errors with `let _ =` on fallible operations. Always handle errors appropriately:
  - Propagate errors with `?` when the calling function should handle them
  - Use `.log_err()` or similar when you need to ignore errors but want visibility
  - Use explicit error handling with `match` or `if let Err(...)` when you need custom logic
  - Example: avoid `let _ = client.request(...).await?;` - use `client.request(...).await?;` instead
* When implementing async operations that may fail, ensure errors propagate to the UI layer so users get meaningful feedback.
* Never create files with `mod.rs` paths - prefer `src/some_module.rs` instead of `src/some_module/mod.rs`.
* When creating new crates, prefer specifying the library root path in `Cargo.toml` using `[lib] path = "...rs"` instead of the default `lib.rs`, to maintain consistent and descriptive naming (e.g., `gpui.rs` or `main.rs`).
* Avoid creative additions unless explicitly requested
* Use full words for variable names (no abbreviations like "q" for "queue")
* Use variable shadowing to scope clones in async contexts for clarity, minimizing the lifetime of borrowed references.
  Example:
  ```rust
  executor.spawn({
      let task_ran = task_ran.clone();
      async move {
          *task_ran.borrow_mut() = true;
      }
  });
  ```

# GPUI

GPUI is the UI framework (entities, windows, async tasks, elements, actions). The `gpui` skill has the full primer, and `gpui-test` covers tests. These traps apply everywhere:

* Never update an entity while it is already being updated: GPUI panics on the double lease. Inside an update closure, use the closure's own `cx`.
* Dropping a `Task` cancels it. Await it, `detach()`/`detach_and_log_err(cx)` it, or store it in a field.
* Call `cx.notify()` when state that affects rendering changes. GPUI also re-renders every visible view on each window redraw, so `render` must stay cheap.
* An element with `.absolute()` and no insets stays at its static position, not at the parent's origin. Add `.top_0().left_0()` or explicit insets.

## GPUI tests

* `#[gpui::test]` functions must return `()`. The macro discards the return value, so a test that returns `Err` passes. Assert or `expect` instead. (#1)
* Prefer GPUI executor timers over `smol::Timer::after(...)` for timeouts, delays, or to drive `run_until_parked()`: use `cx.background_executor().timer(duration).await` (or `cx.background_executor.timer(duration).await` in `TestAppContext`). GPUI's scheduler may not track `smol` timers, which leads to "nothing left to run" when pumping.
* Focus and blur regressions need an active window: activate it, run until parked, and assert it is active before simulating focus changes. An inactive window moves focus handles without firing the events under test. (#6)

# Pull request hygiene

When an agent opens or updates a pull request, it must:

- Use a clear, correctly capitalized, imperative PR title (for example, `Fix crash in project panel`).
- Avoid conventional commit prefixes in PR titles (`fix:`, `feat:`, `docs:`, etc.).
- Avoid trailing punctuation in PR titles.
- Optionally prefix the title with a crate name when one crate is the clear scope (for example, `git_ui: Add history view`).
- Include a `Release Notes:` section as the final section in the PR body.
- Use one bullet under `Release Notes:`:
  - `- Added ...`, `- Fixed ...`, or `- Improved ...` for user-facing changes, or
  - `- N/A` for docs-only and other non-user-facing changes.
- Format release notes exactly with a blank line after the heading, for example:

```
Release Notes:

- N/A
```

# Rules hygiene

This file, the crate `AGENTS.md` files and the skills are read by every agent session. Keep them high-signal: rules are traps to avoid, not maps to follow. Architecture descriptions go stale, and agents can read the code.

- A rule must be non-obvious (someone familiar with the code would still get it wrong), specific enough to act on, and likely to recur.
- A trap found while changing a crate: add it to that crate's `AGENTS.md` in the same PR and cite the PR, so reviewers judge it with the diff.
- A root-level rule (this file): propose it in the PR description under a **"Suggested .rules additions"** heading. The `rules-harvest` skill periodically turns those suggestions into a dedicated PR.
- When the code a rule describes changes, fix or delete the rule in the same PR.
- A new crate rules file is `crates/<crate>/AGENTS.md` with a `CLAUDE.md` symlink beside it (`ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md`), listed under "Crate rules" above.
- Skills live in `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, where Codex reads them. Add a symlink `.claude/skills/<name>` → `../../.agents/skills/<name>` so Claude Code sees them too. Keep frontmatter to `name` and `description`, which both tools read.
